    <p class="eyebrow">Trust & Standards</p>
    <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
    <h2>Evidence first</h2>
    <p>We prioritize primary sources such as FAO, WFP, IPC, World Bank, national statistical agencies, agriculture ministries, climate agencies and peer-reviewed research.</p>
    <h2>Clear attribution</h2>
    <p>Claims from third parties are attributed to the source. We distinguish reporting, official estimates, projections and explanatory analysis.</p>
    <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
    <p>Important data can change. We update articles when material evidence changes and correct material factual errors.</p>
    <h2>Local recommendations, not universal checklists</h2>
    <p>Practical advice must match the place and the problem. Country and locality articles distinguish household needs, farm decisions, market operations and public policy instead of repeating one checklist everywhere. Weather-dependent advice names the issuing agency, forecast date, affected area and forecast period; a national climate signal is not treated as a local forecast.</p>
    <p>We consider storage capacity before recommending stock. Household quantities must fit safe, dry and pest-resistant space, access to clean water, refrigeration and the household budget. Commercial and public storage are assessed separately by commodity, location, condition, rotation, distribution capacity and release rules. A national stock figure is not assumed to be available to every town or household.</p>
    <p>Where local evidence is unavailable, we say so and direct readers to the relevant local authority or extension service rather than inventing a district-level recommendation. Similar topics may link to one canonical practical guide, but they should not copy its instructions into unrelated reporting.</p>
    <h2>Reader-first SEO</h2>
    <p>Search visibility matters, but pages are written for people first. We avoid misleading headlines, duplicate country pages and content created only to capture search traffic. Each article should answer a distinct reader question and offer only the actions supported by its own geography, evidence and audience.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
